.TH "SECURE\-TERMINAL" "1" "January 2020" "secure-terminal" "secure-terminal Manual"
.SH "NAME"
\fBsecure\-terminal\fR \- a terminal where paste is safe by design
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBsecure\-terminal\fR [OPTIONS] [\fB\-\-\fR \fIPROGRAM\fR [\fIARGS\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.]]
.P
\fBsecure\-terminal\fR \fB\-e\fR \fISTRING\fR
.P
\fBsecure\-terminal\fR \fB\-\-reuse\fR [OPTIONS] [\fB\-\-\fR \fIPROGRAM\fR [\fIARGS\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.]]
.P
\fBsecure\-terminal\fR \fBctl\fR [\fB\-\-instance\-group\fR \fINAME\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
secure\-terminal is a small terminal for pasting commands into, and reading text out of, without worrying about invisible characters, bidi overrides or escape sequences\. Its default display mode, \fBdetail\fR, is printable ASCII: a non\-ASCII character is NAMED inline as \fB<U+XXXX NAME>\fR rather than drawn, so a look\-alike reads as its identity\. The other modes trade that differently \-\- \fBreveal\fR shortens the badge to the codepoint, \fBbox\fR replaces the character with one coloured placeholder glyph, and \fBshow\fR renders a printable glyph as itself (tinted by risk class), which is the one mode that puts real non\-ASCII on screen\. What holds in EVERY mode is that no invisible, bidi or control character reaches you unmarked\.
.P
It runs no escape\-sequence parser on program output beyond safe colour and four line\-local edits within the line being written, so a pasted or printed lie cannot reorder what you see or reach a line you have already scrolled past\. Paste is sanitized to ASCII before it reaches the shell\.
.P
Without \fB\-\-\fR or \fB\-e\fR, a shell (from \fB$SHELL\fR, else \fB/bin/bash\fR) is started\. With \fB\-\-\fR the remaining arguments are run as a real argv, with no shell reparse\.
.P
Each launch opens its own independent window and process (see INSTANCES); pass \fB\-\-reuse\fR to add a tab to an already\-running instance instead\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-title\fR \fITITLE\fR
Set the initial tab title\.
.TP
\fB\-\-tui\fR, \fB\-\-no\-tui\fR
Start this tab in TUI mode (interpret escapes through a confined screen model) or in the safe CLI mode\. Overrides the configured default for this tab only\. TUI mode needs \fBpython3\-pyte\fR\.
.TP
\fB\-\-mode\fR \fBbox\fR|\fBshow\fR|\fBreveal\fR|\fBdetail\fR
Initial unicode display mode (see DISPLAY MODES)\.
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-command\fR \fISTRING\fR
Run \fISTRING\fR, shell\-split but without a shell\. Prefer \fB\-\-\fR \fIPROGRAM\fR \fIARGS\fR for a real argv\.
.TP
\fB\-\-tab\fR
Start another per\-tab option group: the options after it configure the next tab\.
.TP
\fB\-\-colors\fR, \fB\-\-no\-colors\fR, \fB\-\-line\-edits\fR, \fB\-\-no\-line\-edits\fR, \fB\-\-bell\fR \fICHANNELS\fR, \fB\-\-osc\fR \fIFEATURE\fR
Per\-tab overrides of the configured defaults for the matching keys (see CONFIGURATION); an administrator \fBlock\fR still wins\. \fB\-\-osc\fR is repeatable\.
.TP
\fB\-\-class\fR \fICLASS\fR
Set the window WM_CLASS / Wayland app\-id, for window\-manager rules\.
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR
Set the window WM_CLASS instance name (X11)\.
.TP
\fB\-\-reuse\fR
Open a new tab in the group's running (primary) instance instead of a new window\. Starts a fresh instance if none is running (see INSTANCES)\.
.TP
\fB\-\-window\fR
Open a new independent window and process (the default)\. The explicit spelling of the default behaviour\.
.TP
\fB\-\-new\-instance\fR
Force a standalone, server\-less process: like the default new window, but it never becomes the group primary and answers no \fBctl\fR or \fB\-\-reuse\fR\.
.P
\fB\-\-reuse\fR, \fB\-\-window\fR and \fB\-\-new\-instance\fR are three mutually exclusive dispositions; at most one may be given\.
.TP
\fB\-\-if\-absent\fR
With \fB\-\-reuse\fR, skip any tab whose command already runs in a tab of the target window, opening only the ones not present\. The open request is idempotent: re\-sending the same batch adds nothing\. Matching is by command (a \fB\-e\fR STRING and its shell\-split argv are equal), so a renamed tab is still matched\. No effect without \fB\-\-reuse\fR\.
.TP
\fB\-\-instance\-group\fR \fINAME\fR
The instance group whose primary \fB\-\-reuse\fR and \fBctl\fR target (default: "default")\. See INSTANCES\.
.TP
\fB\-\-clipboard\-watch\fR, \fB\-\-tray\fR
Run as a tray\-only clipboard sanitizer instead of opening a terminal window\. It watches the system clipboard and, when copied text carries deceptive Unicode (an invisible, bidirectional or control character, or a homoglyph posing as ASCII), offers to replace it with a safe version \-\- flag\-and\-offer, it never rewrites the clipboard on its own\. This protects text later pasted into an editor or any program that does not neutralize Unicode\. It sanitizes the LOCAL clipboard only; it does not alter the Qubes inter\-VM global clipboard (Ctrl+Shift+C / Ctrl+Shift+V)\. See also \fBsclip\fR(1)\.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and exit\.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a usage summary and exit\.
.TP
\fB\-\-\fR \fIPROGRAM\fR [\fIARGS\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.]
Run \fIPROGRAM\fR directly as a real argv (no shell reparse)\. Repeat a leading program per tab where supported\.
.SH "DISPLAY MODES"
secure\-terminal has two rendering modes, chosen per tab:
.TP
CLI mode (default, safe)
A strict, line\-oriented display\. The only escapes honored are safe colour and four line\-local edits (cursor forward/back, cursor\-to\-column, erase\-in\-line), and only within the line being written; vertical and absolute movement is stripped\. A program can redraw its own current line, exactly as a carriage return does, but can never reach back to a line already scrolled past\. Those four are what tab completion needs (Tab makes the shell rewrite the line you are typing in place); \fBline_edits=false\fR, or \fBView > Line editing\fR, turns them off \-\- see the key's entry in the defaults file (see CONFIGURATION) for what that gains and costs\.
.TP
TUI mode (opt\-in)
Escapes are interpreted inside an isolated in\-memory screen model that has no OS reach: it cannot set the window title or touch the system clipboard\. Every on\-screen character is still ASCII/unicode\-filtered and colours keep a contrast guard\. Use it to run full\-screen programs (ssh, an editor, htop) and for the shell's completion menus and progress displays\.
.P
The unicode \fB\-\-mode\fR controls how a non\-ASCII byte in output is shown (the top\-bar label for \fBdetail\fR is "Detail"):
.TP
\fBbox\fR
Replace it with a coloured box, one per character, tinted by risk class\. On copy the box maps to a plain ASCII "_"; a saved transcript instead names each such byte inline (\fB<U+XXXX NAME>\fR), so the record stays lossless\.
.TP
\fBshow\fR
Render the glyph when printable, tinted by risk class so a look\-alike stands out (a homoglyph confusable with ASCII wears a louder colour than honest foreign text)\. Box\-drawing and block\-element glyphs are structural, not deceptive, so they render in the program's own colour\. A non\-ASCII space is shown as a distinct marker and copies as "_", never as a space\. Non\-printable classes (invisible, bidi, control) have no glyph, so they still show as the same coloured box\.
.TP
\fBreveal\fR
Show a \fB<U+XXXX>\fR codepoint badge (hover for its name)\.
.TP
\fBdetail\fR
Like reveal but with the codepoint's Unicode name inline, \fB<U+XXXX NAME>\fR, so a homoglyph reads as its identity rather than a bare number (the safe default)\.
.SH "REVIEW BAR"
When text crossing the terminal boundary would carry unicode or control characters \-\- a paste coming IN, or a selection copied OUT with Ctrl+C \-\- the tab HOLDS it and shows a review bar docked at the bottom; no byte reaches the shell (or the clipboard) until you choose\. The bar shows a one\-line summary, a per\-class breakdown of what is hidden (look\-alikes, invisible/zero\-width, bidirectional controls, control characters, combining marks, box\-drawing, other non\-ASCII), a structure summary (line count, whether it would run on its own, length), and a mirror pane rendering exactly what each choice would deliver\. The held text is editable: trim the risky part and the summary, breakdown and mirror update, and delivery sends exactly the edited buffer (re\-sanitized, so an edit cannot bypass the neutralization)\. Choose Reject, the ASCII form (look\-alikes stripped) or the unicode form (printable non\-ASCII kept)\. A paste never runs on its own: the trailing submit byte is removed so the command waits at the prompt for your own Enter\.
.P
\fBpaste_warn\fR and \fBcopy_warn\fR (each \fBunicode\fR by default; also \fBalways\fR or \fBnever\fR) control when the bar appears in each direction\. The two directions are NOT symmetric, by design:
.IP "\(bu" 4
A paste is inspected as the raw clipboard bytes, in every display mode\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
A Ctrl+C copy is inspected as the ON\-SCREEN selection\. In the box, reveal and detail display modes the selection is already sanitized to ASCII, so nothing risky can reach the clipboard and no review is needed; only in show mode, where real glyphs are kept, does a copy raise the bar\. A mouse selection (the X11 PRIMARY buffer) and drag\-and\-drop are always hard\-stripped to ASCII, so a middle\-click paste of a selection is safe without a review\. This is why a copy may raise the bar while pasting the same text back does not: what reached the clipboard was already safe\.
.IP "" 0
.SH "CONFIGURATION"
Settings are \fBKEY=value\fR lines spread over \fB*\.conf\fR drop\-in files, read from these directories, lowest precedence first:
.IP "" 4
.nf
/usr/lib/secure\-terminal\.d/*\.conf       built\-in vendor defaults
/etc/secure\-terminal\.d/*\.conf           system administrator
/usr/local/etc/secure\-terminal\.d/*\.conf local administrator
~/\.config/secure\-terminal\.d/*\.conf      user (highest precedence)
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The user's own choices are written to \fB~/\.config/secure\-terminal\.d/50_user\.conf\fR\. A privileged directory may \fBlock\fR a key so the user cannot override it\. The built\-in defaults file documents every key\. See also the app's View menu and the global Settings dialog\.
.SH "INSTANCES"
Every launch opens a new independent window in its own process, like konsole or qterminal \-\- a second \fBsecure\-terminal\fR gives you a second window, not a raised first one\. Independent processes keep sessions isolated and re\-read the administrator locks per process\.
.P
Reuse is opt\-in\. \fB\-\-reuse\fR hands the launch to the group's PRIMARY instance, which opens it as a new tab; with no tab options it opens a fresh default tab\. The primary is the FIRST live instance of a group: it owns the group's owner\-only socket and is what \fB\-\-reuse\fR and \fBsecure\-terminal ctl\fR target\. Later launches in the same group run independently and do not disturb it\. If no primary is running, \fB\-\-reuse\fR starts one\.
.P
\fB\-\-instance\-group\fR \fINAME\fR selects the group (socket identity); an instance is still a new window unless \fB\-\-reuse\fR is also given\. \fB\-\-new\-instance\fR forces a standalone process that never becomes a primary and answers no \fBctl\fR or \fB\-\-reuse\fR\.
.SH "REMOTE CONTROL"
\fBsecure\-terminal ctl\fR can list tabs, rename a tab, read a tab's rendered text (\fBdump\-tab\fR), live\-zoom a tab (\fBzoom\fR, no restart) and send text into a tab (\fBsend\-text\fR, as if typed) over an owner\-only socket\. It is an injection/read surface and is OFF by default; it can only be enabled from a privileged configuration directory (\fBremote_control\fR)\.
.SH "FILES"
.TP
\fB~/\.config/secure\-terminal\.d/*\.conf\fR
User configuration (see CONFIGURATION)\.
.TP
\fB~/\.local/state/secure\-terminal/\fR
Restored session: the tab list and each tab's scrollback\.
.TP
\fB/etc/apparmor\.d/secure\-terminal\fR
AppArmor confinement profile\.
.SH "SECURITY"
secure\-terminal does not sandbox the programs you run; if you choose to run something harmful it runs, as in any terminal\. What it protects is VIEWING untrusted data: a crafted file, a program's output, a login banner or a filename can carry escape sequences that, on a normal terminal, act rather than merely display \-\- quietly changing your clipboard (so a later paste inserts text you never copied), drawing a link whose text lies about its target, or putting text onto your input line\. secure\-terminal removes those escapes so reading untrusted output is safe, and sanitizes paste so untrusted text cannot smuggle a hidden command into the shell\.
.P
The safe\-by\-design guarantees rest on the CLI mode: no escape parser, printable\-ASCII\-only display, ASCII\-sanitized paste, and a memory\-safe implementation\. TUI mode is a deliberate, clearly\-indicated, lower\-guarantee mode; the confined screen model still filters every character, and each OSC feature (title, clipboard, hyperlink, colours, \|\.\|\.\|\.) is neutralized unless you enable it (see \fBView > OSC features\fR), at your own risk\. Only run programs you trust in TUI mode\.
